\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Three scale-invariant tests of wordy stabilisation, per mirror pair, averaged across the four 500-sample runs. $\Delta $margin: clean gold-vs-best-distractor margin gap (wordy minus terse). Flip-rate $\downarrow $: relative reduction in argmax-flip rate under perturbation (positive $=$ wordy more stable). Acc $\uparrow $: gain in fraction of perturbations preserving top-$1$ $=$ gold. Bold marks the strongest cell. MCQ pairs (L2/L6, L4/L8) show monotone improvement in $C_{\mathrm  {sem}}$; binary Y/N pairs (L1/L5, L3/L7) are weaker because the $2$-option ceiling dominates per-perturbation metrics. The companion margin-volatility test (variance of the gold-vs-distractor margin under perturbation) is not reported here: when both gold and best distractor sit near the log-prob ceiling, the margin is a difference of two saturated quantities and its noise floor rises in absolute nats even though the underlying decision is more stable (caught and excluded in our scale-invariant audit; see also \cref  {tab:variance-reduction}).}}{14}{table.caption.38}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:cleanboost}{{9}{14}{Three scale-invariant tests of wordy stabilisation, per mirror pair, averaged across the four 500-sample runs. $\Delta $margin: clean gold-vs-best-distractor margin gap (wordy minus terse). Flip-rate $\downarrow $: relative reduction in argmax-flip rate under perturbation (positive $=$ wordy more stable). Acc $\uparrow $: gain in fraction of perturbations preserving top-$1$ $=$ gold. Bold marks the strongest cell. MCQ pairs (L2/L6, L4/L8) show monotone improvement in $\Csem $; binary Y/N pairs (L1/L5, L3/L7) are weaker because the $2$-option ceiling dominates per-perturbation metrics. The companion margin-volatility test (variance of the gold-vs-distractor margin under perturbation) is not reported here: when both gold and best distractor sit near the log-prob ceiling, the margin is a difference of two saturated quantities and its noise floor rises in absolute nats even though the underlying decision is more stable (caught and excluded in our scale-invariant audit; see also \cref {tab:variance-reduction})}{table.caption.38}{}}
\newlabel{tab:cleanboost@cref}{{[table][9][2147483647]9}{[1][13][]14}}
